			<div class="content" id="post-content-2857899">
									

<p>Overall, I liked this one. I have one nitpick, though - an object catapulted away from Earth at escape speed would have a very poor chance of ending up in a stable orbit of anything besides the sun. This article references victims winding up in Jupiter orbit, which is highly unlikely, and also Triton orbit, which is practically impossible (and you mention that <em>multiple</em> subjects have ended up there. For one, both of these objects is really far away, and the likelihood of passing anywhere near them by accident would be absolutely vanishing. Two, you can't generally get into orbit around anything in space just by flying near it - tiny effects due to non-spherical gravitation nonwithstanding, you need to be able to exert another impulse to slow yourself down and get into a closed orbit, or you just end up flying off again. If you meant that this skip actually teleports you such that your position and velocity put you in a stable orbit of certain solar system bodies, fine, but I don't see that in the article - it sounds like you mean that they just flew out into space and <em>happened</em> to get captured by a passing moon of Neptune, which is so unlikely as to be unreasonable.</p>
<p>Other than that, I thought this was a very amusing and well-written article. +1, albeit with some reservation.</p>

