<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;physics</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;hide&nbsp;physics</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Simplistically, gravity forms potential wells, where the potential energy close to the body is much lower than the potential energy far away. As you move towards the central body on a freefall trajectory (e.g. without firing a rocket engine, as I'm assuming these poor D-class are not able to do), you gain kinetic energy (speed) equal to the potential energy you lose, in accordance with conservation of energy. If you are released from rest at a certain height above a body, your total energy (assuming you don't actually strike the body) will stay the same, and you'll end up back at the altitude where you started. Stable orbits around a body can only exist for a certain range of total energies at a given altitude; if your energy is too large, you'll fly away no matter what, and if it's too small, you'll fall into the central body.<br />
The result of this is that, if you come in from a large distance away from the body in question, your total energy (if not altered by drag, tidal forces, N-body effects, etc) will allow you to return to the height you started from, which is, in the case of these hammers, all the way back at Earth, and you won't get into a closed orbit. Since energy can't be created or destroyed, you have to find a way to get rid of it in order to get it down into the range where your orbit will be stable and prevent yourself from flying off again. Spacecraft do this either by firing rockets or by dipping low into the atmosphere and hoping their heatshields hold. D-class ejected from the Earth by surprise have no such options, so in a truly overwhelming majority of cases, they're going to end up crashed, burnt, or orbiting the sun - capturing into orbit of another solar system body is a very edge case.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Captured satellites are either the result of the non-spherical gravitation effects I mentioned before, or interference from an additional body. Tidal forces on large bodies tend to drag down their orbits at the periapsis and make them more circular, which can bring them into closed orbits if they pass the right way. It's not something that can happen at all if you assume point masses and large distances, so if you're talking about human bodies and high orbit around Jupiter, tidal capture simply isn't happening. Multi-body interference is more likely to work, but it's the sort of thing that will only happen with extremely narrow launch windows and extremely specific trajectories. The way the article is currently written, it sounds like &quot;High-orbit around Jupiter&quot; is assumed to just be some altitude away from Earth, which is wrong. As I said, I don't mind if being teleported or dragged into orbit of various solar system bodies is part of the anomaly, but I sure don't see that written in the article.</p>

							</div>

								
